= What a container actually is
: A container is a process with a restricted view of one machine — not a small computer of its own.

~ package  green
~ good     green
~ problem  red
~ system   blue
~ inert    grey

symbol whale = container

// The Docker board this language grew from, and the reference for the house
// style. Labels are written the way they are meant to be read: sentence case,
// one hand, hierarchy from size. Only scene 3 draws a sequence — the rest is
// one diagram being pointed at, changed and added to.

--- 1 [6 beats] "What a virtual machine is"
// The scene the whole style is set by. A labelled container on the left, a
// column of icon-and-line properties down the middle, the verdict on the right.
box #vm Virtual machine ~problem |1
  box #hyp Hypervisor / one real machine, several pretend ones |2
  box #guest Guest OS / its own kernel, its own drivers |3

column #costs |4
  sym shield : Good isolation ~good |4
  sym warehouse : Heavy — gigabytes on disk |5
  sym stopwatch : About a minute to boot |5
  sym server : A whole OS for every program |6

column |6
  arr #many -, -, -, -, -, -, - ~problem |6
  sym sad face |6

--- 2 [6 beats] "A process wearing a costume"
box #host The host machine ~system |2
  box #kernel One shared kernel ~system |2*

box #container An ordinary process ~package |2*
shares container kernel : nothing is emulated, nothing is virtualised |5

group #hidden What the kernel hides from it ~inert |3
  box Its own filesystem |4
  box Its own network |4
  box Its own process table |4

sym processor |2
sym layers |3
img data centre server rack photograph |4
callout the kernel is lying to it, consistently ~package |5

--- 3 [6 beats] "Recipe, image, container"
flow horizontal #build ~package
  Dockerfile / the recipe |2
  Image / read-only, frozen |3
  Registry / anyone can pull it |4
  Container / a running process |5

code FROM python:3.12 / COPY . /app / RUN pip install -r requirements.txt / CMD ["python", "app.py"] |2*

box #same The same bytes / on your laptop and in production ~package |6

sym wrench |2
sym container |5
img cargo ship loading at port photograph |6

--- 4 [4 beats] "Frozen, and therefore shareable"
carry #same

stk #layers ~package |2
  Your app — one thin writable layer, per container |4
  pip packages — read-only, shared |2
  python:3.12 — read-only, shared |2
  debian base — read-only, shared |2

hl #layers.top |4
box #ten Ten containers [1 image · 10 thin layers] ~package |3

sym layers |2
sym disk |3
img warehouse shelving stacked high photograph |4
callout ten containers do not cost ten copies ~package |4

--- 5 [4 beats] "The two doors you open yourself"
carry #container

label Two things it does not keep to itself |1

box #volume Volume / host storage, mounted in ~system |2
mounts volume container : data outlives the container |2

box #port Published port [8080 → 80] ~system |3
maps port container : one door, opened on purpose |3

sym folder |2
sym cable |3
img ship container with its doors open photograph |3
callout everything else is hidden unless you asked for it ~package |4

--- 6 [4 beats] "Not a small computer"
carry #container

transform #container |2
  A process with a restricted view [1 kernel · 1 image · 0 operating systems]

group #truth What is actually going on ~system |3
  box A restricted view
  box A frozen image
  box A shared kernel

focus #truth |3
sym processor |2
sym scales |3
img single lit window in an office block photograph |4
callout everything fast about containers follows from what they do not do ~package |4
